instead of PERIOD", "summary": "Repro for the period failure (period_fail.log in [run:run-0001]) against [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. It holds duty=3 constant, measures the clock cycles between successive rising edges of pwm three times, and expects PERIOD=8 each time. Reading the RTL, the cnt == PERIOD-2 wrap should give 7, so all three checks are expected to fail. The testbench prints the NIRMAAN-SIM verdict once before $finish.", "entry": "pwm_repro_tb"}, {"path": "tb/pwm_pulse_width_repro_tb.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro: pwm_gen high time is duty+1 cycles; duty 0 still pulses", "summary": "Repro for the pulse-width failure (pulse_width_fail.log in [run:run-0001]) against [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. With duty=3 held constant, it measures three high pulses and expects a width of 3 each. With duty=0, it expects pwm to stay low for 3 periods. Reading the RTL, the `cnt <= duty_now` compare should give widths of 4 and one high cycle per period at duty 0. The testbench prints the NIRMAAN-SIM verdict once before $finish.", "entry": "pwm_repro_tb"}, {"path": "tb/pwm_duty_update_repro_tb.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro: pwm_gen applies a mid-period duty change immediately", "summary": "Repro for the duty-update failure (duty_update_fail.log in [run:run-0001]) against [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. It runs at duty=2 and finds a period start. Three cycles in, after pwm has fallen, it raises duty to 6. It then checks that pwm stays low for the next 3 cycles, as the RTL header says a mid-period change applies only in the next period. The check window is shorter than even the faulty 7-cycle period, so it isolates the duty_now-vs-duty_q defect. The testbench prints the NIRMAAN-SIM verdict once before $finish.", "entry": "pwm_repro_tb"}], "tool_runs": ["run:run-0001"], "claims": ["The three logs map to the defects by file name: period_fail.log to the PERIOD-2 wrap, pulse_width